# Valgrind suppressions for the WaveDB-backed frame suites (test_frame.cpp,
# later test_loop.cpp).
#
# WaveDB (deps/wavedb, statically linked) launches its worker pool on every
# concurrent-mode database open (sync_only=0 is the frame store's ground
# state); database_destroy shuts down AND joins those threads cleanly. The
# only blocks valgrind still attributes to them are glibc's nptl stack-cache
# TLS bookkeeping (allocate_stack/_dl_allocate_tls) — a known glibc/valgrind
# false-positive class for joined threads: the stack cache retains the TLS
# blocks after pthread_join and valgrind misclassifies the cached blocks as
# possibly-lost. No WaveDB- or SecretAgent-owned frame is in the lost part of
# any of these stacks — the only named frame is the work_pool_launch call
# site, matching the documented-external-owner precedent of vg-cpython.supp.
#
# One entry with an ellipsis covers all four observed contexts (the middle
# glibc frames vary across record shapes: 1 block, 1 block, 2 blocks).
{
   glibc-nptl-stack-cache-tls-behind-wavedb-workers
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: possible
   fun:calloc
   ...
   fun:work_pool_launch
   ...
}